@@ -1184,6 +1186,35 @@ These limits are approximate, so `vmagent` can underflow or overflow them by a s
 
 See also [cardinality explorer docs](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/single-server-victoriametrics/#cardinality-explorer).
 
+## Limiting the number of scrape targets per job
+
+By default, `vmagent` scrapes every target discovered for a `job_name`, no matter how many targets a service
+discovery mechanism (Kubernetes, Consul, EC2, DNS, etc.) returns for it. This can be a problem when service
+discovery briefly misbehaves because of misconfiguration (for example, an overly broad Kubernetes selector or
+Consul query) and suddenly returns an excessive number of targets for a single job, which can spike `vmagent`
+CPU and memory usage and degrade scraping for every other job running on the same `vmagent` instance.
+
+The number of simultaneously scraped targets per `job_name` can be limited in the following places:
+
+* Via `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag. This limit is applied individually to every
+  `job_name` defined in the file pointed to by `-promscrape.config`.
+* Via `max_scrape_targets` option at [scrape_config](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs) section.
+  It overrides `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` on a per-`scrape_config` basis. If `max_scrape_targets` is
+  set to `0`, then no limit is applied to the given `scrape_config`, even if `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob`
+  is set.
+
+By default, there is no limit, so existing setups are unaffected.
+
+The limit is applied uniformly no matter which service discovery mechanism(s) supplied the targets for the job.
+When the number of discovered targets for a job exceeds the limit, `vmagent` keeps scraping the targets it
+already started and stops considering the rest, instead of crashing, rejecting the whole job, or scraping
+everything. Once discovery returns fewer targets than the limit again, scraping resumes normally.
+
+Targets excluded because of the limit are shown at the `http://vmagent:8429/targets` and
+`http://vmagent:8429/service-discovery` pages (with the `exceeds job target limit` reason at
+`/service-discovery`), and are counted in the `vm_promscrape_scrape_pool_targets{scrape_job="<job_name>", status="excluded"}`
+metric, so it is easy to spot which jobs are currently affected and roughly how many targets are being left out.
+
 ## Monitoring
 
 `vmagent` exports various metrics in Prometheus exposition format at `http://vmagent-host:8429/metrics` page.
